m=core.nocustody.bound;

i=db:std.db;
i=str:std.str;
i=crypto:std.crypto;
i=jx:shared.jsonobj;
i=t:shared.types;
i=na:core.nocustody.artifact;
i=nr:core.nocustody.request;
i=ns:core.nocustody.session;
i=nl:core.nocustody.limits;
i=op:core.privacy.orgpolicy;
i=alert:core.governance.alert;

(* NC1.10. A per-session bound on the query-shaped channel.

   An artifact never carries a row to the model, but its RESULT can. Whoever shapes the
   artifacts and sees the results across several turns can learn cell values a piece at a
   time: "count where note = X" answers whether X is in the column, and enough of those
   enumerate it (findings section 11). core.nocustody.artifact makes each query safe to run.
   This module limits what a SEQUENCE of them can release, per session, and records every
   release so a later dispatch can account for it (spec 5.5, C5, C6).

   Two limits, the two session budgets of docs/specifications/artifact-contract.md section 8,
   with that table's names and default values, each checked before any result is released:

   1. SESSION_CELL_BUDGET (cellbudget): cells released per session. This is the one that
      bounds the unfiltered channel: min, max, distinct and count_by put data values straight
      into a result, and the only thing that limits a session built from them is volume.
      Rows are counted too (spec 5.5 session_rows_returned_cumulative) but not budgeted
      separately: the contract has no session row budget, and a row is at least one cell.
   2. SESSION_PREDICATE_REPEAT_LIMIT (probelimit): filtered runs per filter column per session.
      A filter is an equality test on one column with a value the artifact chose, so a run of
      them against the same column with different values is the value-probing pattern. The
      count is per filter COLUMN, not per full predicate, so changing the op or the aggregated
      column between probes does not start a new count. The contract counts DISTINCT values;
      this counts every filtered run, which is never fewer. The difference is deliberate: the
      value is never stored (see core.storage.migrations, nc_artifact_runs), so a repeat
      cannot be told from a new guess, and the count errs towards refusing.

   A result that would take the session over budget is computed locally and then withheld:
   the refusal names the limit, and the ledger records it with zero released. If the ledger
   cannot be read or written, nothing is released. A bound whose own record failed is not a
   bound.

   NC1.13 adds two things. First, the session is one loke issued (core.nocustody.session):
   an id loke did not issue, or one that has expired, is refused before the ledger is read.
   Second, a bound across sessions, because issuing ids does not stop a caller asking for
   more of them. Over a rolling INSTALLATION_WINDOW (24 hours), across EVERY session on this
   installation, the same two limits apply again at the same values:

   3. INSTALLATION_CELL_BUDGET (installcellbudget): cells released in the window, all sessions
      together, 50 000.
   4. INSTALLATION_PREDICATE_REPEAT_LIMIT (installprobelimit): filtered runs per filter column
      in the window, all sessions together, 20.

   So a probing sequence spread over rotated ids is refused at the same count as one in a
   single session, and a caller gains no cells by rotating. The session limits are checked
   first, so a single session sees its own refusal codes (cell_budget, value_probing) and a
   rotating caller sees the installation's (installation_cell_budget, cross_session_probing).
   It is per installation, not per caller or per origin: loke cannot tell callers apart (every
   accepted request is a loopback one with no foreign Origin), so one caller's use counts
   against every other's. That is the cost: legitimate sessions share one budget per window,
   and heavy ordinary use across many sessions is refused where it was not before.

   WHAT THIS DOES NOT BOUND, and the claim must say so:
   - Time. The installation limits are a rate, not a total: a caller that waits out the window
     gets another 50 000 cells and another 20 guesses per column. Over a week that is seven
     windows' worth.
   - The dataset. The probe key is the filter column's NAME. The same name in two datasets
     shares one count, and a column renamed by the caller (who supplies the headers) starts a
     new one. The caller holds the rows anyway; the bound is on what a model shaping the
     artifacts can learn through a caller that forwards them.
   - Concurrency. The check reads the ledger and the record writes it, not in one transaction,
     so requests racing on separate server workers can each pass a check the other would have
     failed, overshooting by at most the number of concurrent requests. That was already true
     of the per-session limits.
   - Information per cell. The budget counts cells, not bits: a SUM or COUNT over a filter
     that isolates one row is one cell and can carry that row's value exactly.
   - Probing a column without a filter, for example through group-by labels. That is bounded
     only by the cell budget.
   - A binary search on a numeric column. The executor has no range filter today, so an
     equality filter is the only probe; a range operator added later needs its own count.
   The values are the contract's unmeasured placeholders, not a measured operating point. No
   benchmark for this channel exists to measure them against.

   NC1.10 (d): the four limits are read from config.toml's [nocustody] table on every run
   (core.nocustody.limits), and config.toml can only make them tighter. A value above the
   contract's default, or one that cannot be used, refuses every run as limits_unusable,
   naming the key, until it is fixed. The functions below are the defaults.

   NC1.14: the organisation policy (loke.policy.json, F3b.8) may set a ceiling on each of the
   four; the limit in force is the lowest of the default, config.toml and the policy, and
   runat reads both files on every run (inforce). A config.toml value above a policy ceiling,
   or a policy file that cannot be applied, refuses every run as org_policy_violation (503),
   naming both keys; it is not clamped (core.nocustody.limits says why). Surfaced as F3b.8
   surfaces the pipeline's: a governance alert (kind privacy_org_policy) for each refusal and
   each run the policy's ceiling lowered a limit for, and a row in this ledger, the one
   POST /api/artifact writes, with verdict refused and code org_policy_violation, or verdict
   org_policy_override. Setting names and numbers only: never a value, label or filter. *)

t=$boundedresult{
  ok:bool;
  code:str;
  err:str;
  labels:@str;
  values:@str;
  sessionrows:i64;
  sessioncells:i64
};

(* SESSION_CELL_BUDGET, artifact-contract section 8 default. Cells a session may be released
   in total. *)
f=cellbudget():i64{
  <nl.defaultsessioncells()
};

(* SESSION_PREDICATE_REPEAT_LIMIT, artifact-contract section 8 default. Filtered runs a session
   may execute against one filter column; the next is refused as value probing. *)
f=probelimit():i64{
  <nl.defaultsessionprobes()
};

(* INSTALLATION_WINDOW, in milliseconds: the rolling window the installation-wide limits sum
   over. 24 hours, the same as core.nocustody.session's TTL, so no session outlives one. *)
f=installwindowms():i64{
  <86400000
};

(* INSTALLATION_CELL_BUDGET and INSTALLATION_PREDICATE_REPEAT_LIMIT: the session limits'
   values, applied across all sessions in the window, so rotating ids buys nothing. *)
f=installcellbudget():i64{
  <cellbudget()
};

f=installprobelimit():i64{
  <probelimit()
};

(* The longest session id accepted. It is an opaque key, not content. *)
f=maxsessionlen():i64{
  <128
};

f=digest(text:str):str{
  <crypto.tohex(crypto.sha256(str.tobytes(text)))
};

(* spec 5.5 predicate_fingerprint: filter column, op and column, with the filter value
   excluded. Built from names only; the "loke/nc1.10" prefix keeps these digests from
   colliding with any other SHA-256 of the same names in this store. *)
f=fingerprint(a:$artifact):str{
  <digest("loke/nc1.10/predicate|\(a.filtercol)|\(a.op)|\(a.column)")
};

(* The per-column probe key, or "" for an artifact with no filter. *)
f=probekey(a:$artifact):str{
  if(str.len(a.filtercol)==0){ <"" };
  <digest("loke/nc1.10/probe|\(a.filtercol)")
};

(* Cells a result releases. A scalar op releases one value and an empty label. A grouped op
   releases a data label and a value per row. distinct releases the label only. *)
f=cellsof(op:str;rows:i64):i64{
  if(na.scalarop(op)){ <rows };
  if(str.eq(op;"distinct")){ <rows };
  <rows*2
};

f=scalar(sql:str;params:@str):i64{
  <mt db.one(sql;params) {
    $ok:r db.rowi64(r;"n");
    $err:e 0-1
  }
};

(* Session totals, and the probe count for one column. -1 when the ledger cannot be read,
   which every caller treats as a refusal. *)
f=sessionrows(session:str):i64{
  <scalar("SELECT COALESCE(SUM(rows_released),0) AS n FROM nc_artifact_runs WHERE session_id=?";@(session))
};

f=sessioncells(session:str):i64{
  <scalar("SELECT COALESCE(SUM(cells_released),0) AS n FROM nc_artifact_runs WHERE session_id=?";@(session))
};

f=probecount(session:str;key:str):i64{
  <scalar("SELECT COUNT() AS n FROM nc_artifact_runs WHERE session_id=? AND probe_key=? AND verdict='executed'";@(session;key))
};

(* NC1.13. Cells released, and filtered runs on one column, in the window ending at `nowms`,
   across every session. -1 when the ledger cannot be read. A row from before migration 38 has
   no occurred_ms and is outside every window. *)
f=installcells(nowms:i64):i64{
  <scalar("SELECT COALESCE(SUM(cells_released),0) AS n FROM nc_artifact_runs WHERE occurred_ms > CAST(? AS INTEGER)";@(str.fromint(nowms-installwindowms())))
};

f=installprobecount(key:str;nowms:i64):i64{
  <scalar("SELECT COUNT() AS n FROM nc_artifact_runs WHERE probe_key=? AND verdict='executed' AND occurred_ms > CAST(? AS INTEGER)";@(key;str.fromint(nowms-installwindowms())))
};

(* spec 5.5 predicate_repeat_count: executed runs of this exact fingerprint in the session. *)
f=repeatcount(session:str;fp:str):i64{
  <scalar("SELECT COUNT() AS n FROM nc_artifact_runs WHERE session_id=? AND predicate_fingerprint=? AND verdict='executed'";@(session;fp))
};

(* spec 5.5 budget_refusals, summed over the session. *)
f=refusalcount(session:str):i64{
  <scalar("SELECT COUNT() AS n FROM nc_artifact_runs WHERE session_id=? AND verdict='refused'";@(session))
};

f=record(session:str;a:$artifact;verdict:str;code:str;rows:i64;cells:i64;nowms:i64):bool{
  <mt db.exec("INSERT INTO nc_artifact_runs (session_id, op, predicate_fingerprint, probe_key, verdict, refusal_code, rows_released, cells_released, occurred_ms) VALUES (?, ?, ?, NULLIF(?,''), ?, NULLIF(?,''), CAST(? AS INTEGER), CAST(? AS INTEGER), CAST(? AS INTEGER))";@(session;a.op;fingerprint(a);probekey(a);verdict;code;str.fromint(rows);str.fromint(cells);str.fromint(nowms))) {
    $ok:n true;
    $err:e false
  }
};

f=refused(code:str;reason:str;srows:i64;scells:i64):$boundedresult{
  <$boundedresult{ok:false;code:code;err:reason;labels:@();values:@();sessionrows:srows;sessioncells:scells}
};

(* Records the refusal, then returns it. The refusal stands whether or not the record could
   be written: failing to record a refusal never turns it into a release. *)
f=refuse(session:str;a:$artifact;verdict:str;code:str;reason:str;srows:i64;scells:i64;nowms:i64):$boundedresult{
  record(session;a;verdict;code;0;0;nowms);
  <refused(code;reason;srows;scells)
};

(* The one entry point, at the current time. *)
f=run(session:str;a:$artifact;headers:@str;rows:@(@str)):$boundedresult{
  <runat(session;a;headers;rows;t.nowms())
};

(* NC1.14. The limits in force now: config.toml under the organisation policy's ceilings, both
   files read on this call. *)
f=inforce():$nclimits{
  <nl.currentunder(op.nocustodyceilings(op.load(op.policypath())))
};

(* At a given time, under the limits config.toml and the organisation policy set now. *)
f=runat(session:str;a:$artifact;headers:@str;rows:@(@str);nowms:i64):$boundedresult{
  let lim=inforce();
  let out=runwith(session;a;headers;rows;nowms;lim);
  surfacepolicy(session;lim;out;nowms);
  <out
};

f=policyalertkind():str{ <"privacy_org_policy" };

f=policyalert(severity:str;msg:str):bool{
  <alert.send(alert.defaultconfig();alert.makealert(policyalertkind();severity;"privacy organisation policy: \(msg)"))
};

(* A ledger row for what the organisation policy did to a run: no op, no fingerprint, no probe
   key, nothing released. So the trail shows which artifact runs the policy acted on. *)
f=recordpolicy(session:str;verdict:str;code:str;nowms:i64):bool{
  <mt db.exec("INSERT INTO nc_artifact_runs (session_id, op, predicate_fingerprint, probe_key, verdict, refusal_code, rows_released, cells_released, occurred_ms) VALUES (?, '', '', NULL, ?, NULLIF(?,''), 0, 0, CAST(? AS INTEGER))";@(session;verdict;code;str.fromint(nowms))) {
    $ok:n true;
    $err:e false
  }
};

f=overrideverdict():str{ <"org_policy_override" };

(* NC1.14. F3b.8's surfacing, for an artifact run: a refusal by the policy is a critical alert
   and a refused row; a run whose limits the policy lowered is a warn alert and an override row.
   Nothing for a run refused before the limits were looked at (no session loke issued). *)
f=surfacepolicy(session:str;lim:$nclimits;out:$boundedresult;nowms:i64):bool{
  if(ns.issessioncode(out.code)){ <false }el{};
  if(str.eq(out.code;nl.policycode())){
    policyalert("critical";"\(lim.error); the artifact run is refused");
    <recordpolicy(session;"refused";nl.policycode();nowms)
  }el{};
  if(lim.notes.len==0){ <false }el{};
  policyalert("warn";"\(nl.policyname()) overrode the no-custody limits in config.toml: \(nl.notestext(lim))");
  <recordpolicy(session;overrideverdict();"";nowms)
};

(* Check the session is one loke issued, check the limits can be applied, validate, check the
   session's ledger and then the installation's window, run, check the result against what is
   left of both, record, release. `nowms` and the limits are parameters so a test can move the
   clock and set the limits; production passes the time and config.toml's. *)
f=runwith(session:str;a:$artifact;headers:@str;rows:@(@str);nowms:i64;lim:$nclimits):$boundedresult{
  if(str.len(session)>maxsessionlen()){ <refused("session_unissued";ns.reason("session_unissued");0;0) };
  let issued=ns.checkat(session;nowms);
  if(str.len(issued)>0){ <refused(issued;ns.reason(issued);0;0) };
  if(str.len(lim.error)>0){ <refused(lim.code;nl.reason(lim);0;0) };
  let cb=lim.sessioncells;
  let pl=lim.sessionprobes;
  let icb=lim.installcells;
  let ipl=lim.installprobes;
  let invalid=na.validate(a;headers);
  if(str.len(invalid)>0){ <refuse(session;a;"rejected";"invalid";invalid;0;0;nowms) };
  let usedrows=sessionrows(session);
  let usedcells=sessioncells(session);
  let allcells=installcells(nowms);
  if(usedrows<0 || usedcells<0 || allcells<0){ <refused("ledger_unavailable";"the session ledger could not be read, so nothing is released";0;0) };
  if(usedcells>=cb){
    <refuse(session;a;"refused";"cell_budget";"this session has used its budget of \(str.fromint(cb)) result cells (NC1.10)";usedrows;usedcells;nowms)
  };
  if(allcells>=icb){
    <refuse(session;a;"refused";"installation_cell_budget";"this installation has released its budget of \(str.fromint(icb)) result cells across all sessions in the last 24 hours (NC1.13); a new session does not reset it";usedrows;usedcells;nowms)
  };
  let key=probekey(a);
  if(str.len(key)>0){
    let probes=probecount(session;key);
    let allprobes=installprobecount(key;nowms);
    if(probes<0 || allprobes<0){ <refused("ledger_unavailable";"the session ledger could not be read, so nothing is released";usedrows;usedcells) };
    if(probes>=pl){
      <refuse(session;a;"refused";"value_probing";"refused as value probing: this session has already filtered on this column \(str.fromint(probes)) times, the limit is \(str.fromint(pl)) (NC1.10)";usedrows;usedcells;nowms)
    }el{};
    if(allprobes>=ipl){
      <refuse(session;a;"refused";"cross_session_probing";"refused as value probing across sessions: this column has been filtered on \(str.fromint(allprobes)) times in the last 24 hours across all sessions, the limit is \(str.fromint(ipl)) (NC1.13); a new session does not reset it";usedrows;usedcells;nowms)
    }el{}
  }el{};
  let result=na.run(a;headers;rows);
  if(result.ok==false){ <refuse(session;a;"rejected";"query_failed";result.err;usedrows;usedcells;nowms) };
  let n=result.labels.len;
  let c=cellsof(a.op;n);
  if(usedcells+c>cb){
    <refuse(session;a;"refused";"cell_budget";"this result has \(str.fromint(c)) cells and the session has \(str.fromint(cb-usedcells)) left of its \(str.fromint(cb)) (NC1.10); it was computed locally and withheld";usedrows;usedcells;nowms)
  };
  if(allcells+c>icb){
    <refuse(session;a;"refused";"installation_cell_budget";"this result has \(str.fromint(c)) cells and the installation has \(str.fromint(icb-allcells)) left of its \(str.fromint(icb)) across all sessions in the last 24 hours (NC1.13); it was computed locally and withheld";usedrows;usedcells;nowms)
  };
  if(record(session;a;"executed";"";n;c;nowms)==false){
    <refused("ledger_unavailable";"the release could not be recorded, so it was withheld";usedrows;usedcells)
  };
  <$boundedresult{ok:true;code:"";err:"";labels:result.labels;values:result.values;sessionrows:usedrows+n;sessioncells:usedcells+c}
};

(* NC1.10. A refusal names its code, so a caller can tell a spent budget from a bad artifact
   without parsing the prose. *)
f=boundedjson(b:$boundedresult):str{
  if(b.ok==false){ <"{\"ok\":false,\"refusal\":\(jx.escape(b.code)),\"error\":\(jx.escape(b.err))}" };
  <"{\"ok\":true,\"labels\":\(nr.strarrayjson(b.labels)),\"values\":\(nr.strarrayjson(b.values)),\"session_rows_released\":\(str.fromint(b.sessionrows)),\"session_cells_released\":\(str.fromint(b.sessioncells))}"
};

(* The status for a refusal: 400 for a request that could not be bounded (no session, or one
   loke did not issue or that has expired), 429 for a spent budget or a probing sequence, in
   one session or across them, 503 when the ledger failed or config.toml's limits cannot be
   applied (NC1.10 (d)) or the organisation policy refuses them (NC1.14), 422 otherwise. *)
f=boundedstatus(b:$boundedresult):i64{
  if(b.ok){ <200 };
  if(ns.issessioncode(b.code)){ <400 };
  if(str.eq(b.code;"cell_budget") || str.eq(b.code;"value_probing")){ <429 };
  if(str.eq(b.code;"installation_cell_budget") || str.eq(b.code;"cross_session_probing")){ <429 };
  if(str.eq(b.code;"ledger_unavailable") || str.eq(b.code;"limits_unusable") || str.eq(b.code;nl.policycode())){ <503 };
  <422
};
